{"title":"Common multiple","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>common multiple</b> of two or more <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Positive_integer\" title=\"Positive integer\">positive integers</a> is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Multiple\" title=\"Multiple\">multiple</a> common to those numbers.  Any <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Finite\" title=\"Finite\">finite</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Set\" title=\"Set\">set</a> of positive integers have an <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinite\" title=\"Infinite\">infinite</a> number of common multiples.\n</p><p>Every common multiple of a set of integers is a multiple of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Least_common_multiple\" title=\"Least common multiple\">least common multiple</a> of those integers.\n</p><p>For example, the least common multiple of 6, 10 and 15 is 30, and the common multiples of 6, 10 and 15 are exactly equal to the multiples of 30.  (As a result of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Poset\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Poset\">poset</a> theory, this says that in the poset of positive integers ordered by the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Divisor\" title=\"Divisor\">divisor</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Relation\" title=\"Relation\">relation</a>, <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Least_upper_bound\" title=\"Least upper bound\">least upper bounds</a> exist for any finite set.)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Common_multiple&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Common_divisor\" title=\"Common divisor\">Common divisor</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017235335\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU 